
PLATFORM: Newsletter section
- Plain text output, no markdown headers or formatting
- 300-500 words total, MINIMUM 300 WORDS, count your words before submitting
- If your draft is under 300 words: add a concrete example or outcome from the source. Use a number, price, date or result only if it appears in the source content or the brand facts above. If there is none, make the point with a concrete detail in plain words instead. Never invent figures, prices, dates, customers or events.
- One main idea per section
- Short paragraphs of 2-3 sentences
- A personal anecdote only if the source or brand facts contain one; otherwise a concrete example from them
- Clear takeaway or next step at the end of the section
- End with a specific action the reader can take this week

STRUCTURE
1. Opening paragraph: the one idea of this section and why the reader should care now, in a sentence or two that a subscriber can act on or reject quickly.
2. Development: three or four short paragraphs. Explain the idea, give the reasoning, then show it with a concrete example from the source or brand facts. Cover one likely doubt or common mistake.
3. Takeaway: one short paragraph that states what the reader now knows or can do differently.
4. Action: one specific step the reader can take this week, small enough to start in a single sitting.
- Write like a knowledgeable friend writing to one subscriber. Plain sentences, no headers, no bullet symbols, no bold markers.

ANTI-GENERIC
- No opening that greets the reader or announces what the section will do.
- Each paragraph adds a new fact, reason or example. Cut any paragraph that only repeats the previous one.
- The action must name what to do and where, not "reflect on this" or "think about your goals".

GOOD: an opening that names the idea, a worked example in plain words, a doubt answered, then an action such as "This week, open your last three replies and mark where the answer came late."
WEAK: "Hope you are well! Today we are talking about productivity. It is very important. Try to be more productive this week."
